;; GENERATED MCP tool-descriptor manifest — do NOT hand-edit.
;; Regenerate from the live tool registry (see the server's
;; descriptor-manifest generator). Drift-checked in CI: adding /
;; removing / renaming a tool turns the gate red until this is
;; regenerated. Shared serialiser: re-frame.mcp-base.descriptor-manifest.
;; Same generate-then-drift-check shape as the api-manifest generator.
{:meta {:server :re-frame2-pair-mcp, :tool-count 30}
 :tools
 [{:name "describe-image" :description "Describe the image generation a frame runs — which registrations it selected and where each came from (EP-0023) — since the same (kind, id) can resolve differently per frame. Returns {:ok? true :frame <id> :images [...] :kinds [...] :counts {<kind> N ...}}. A frame made without :images returns :live-store? true, :images [] and :ambiguous [{:kind k :id id :namespaces [...]} ...], one per id more than one namespace registers, left out of :counts. `include-ns true` adds :registrations {[kind id] <coordinate>}, naming the source that won each. Omit `frame` for the operating frame; a multi-frame session with none selected returns :ambiguous-frame. Drill one id with handler-meta {:frame ... :kind ... :id ...}. Example: {:frame \":main\"} -> {:ok? true :frame :main :images [:app/img] :kinds [:event :sub] :counts {:event 12 :sub 8}}." :input-keys ["build" "cache" "frame" "include-ns" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 600}
  {:name "discover-app" :description "Verify the shadow-cljs nREPL is reachable and the re-frame2-pair runtime preload landed, and report a health summary. Run this first every session. Omit `build` when exactly one build is running (it is auto-selected and noted), or pass `port` to resolve the build serving a browser URL. Returns {:ok? true :build-id ... :frames [...] :app-frames [...] :freshness {...}}. Check :freshness :liveness BEFORE trusting reads: :stale-build means reload the page, :no-runtime means no tab is connected. A :runtime-count above 1 adds :warning :several-runtimes — every op reaches one tab, and no argument picks another. A failed probe names its cause with a :hint: :reason :nrepl-unreachable, :build-not-running, :no-runtime-connected or :runtime-loaded-but-preload-missing. Example: {} -> {:ok? true :build-id :app :auto-selected-build :app :frames [:rf/default] :freshness {:liveness :fresh ...}}." :input-keys ["build" "cache" "max-tokens" "port"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 200}
  {:name "dispatch" :description "Fire a re-frame2 event, tagged :origin :pair. `event` is an EDN vector, parsed once and echoed under :resolved; its id is validated against the live :event registrar, and an unknown id returns :reason :unknown-id with :nearest matches and does NOT dispatch. Non-vector or unreadable input returns :reason :not-an-event-vector or :invalid-event-edn, and host source is refused (use eval-cljs). By default the dispatch runs synchronously and returns its consequence — :epoch-id, :db-changed?, :changed-paths, :effects-fired, :no-op? and a :cascade-summary (:event-id, :db-diff, :fx-fired, sub and render counts, :outcome) — so a no-op is visible. Modes: `queued` returns the async ack (:settled? false until the cascade drains; poll watch-epochs); `trace` adds the full :rf/epoch-record; `await-render` resolves after the DOM paints (:rf.error/dispatch-await-render-timeout after `timeout-ms`); `settle` flushes renders synchronously and returns the settled :epoch with its :render-events, and wins over the others. PRIVACY: the epoch `trace` and `settle` return crosses re-frame.core/project-egress, so declared-sensitive app-db slots read :rf/redacted; `include-sensitive true` keeps them, honoured only when the server was launched with --allow-sensitive-reads. `cofx` scripts recordable coeffects for a deterministic run, and `replay true` re-presents them under the strict mint policy, where a missing fact fails :rf.error/missing-required-cofx. To replay a retained epoch, replay-epoch re-supplies its event, cofx and overrides from the id alone. Example: {:event \"[:cart/checkout]\"} -> {:ok? true :mode :sync :epoch-id 7 :resolved [:cart/checkout] :db-changed? true :changed-paths [[:cart]] :effects-fired [:dispatch] :no-op? false :cascade-summary {...}}." :input-keys ["await-render" "build" "cofx" "event" "frame" "fx-overrides" "include-sensitive" "interceptor-overrides" "max-tokens" "queued" "replay" "settle" "sync" "timeout-ms" "trace"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["event"] :output? true :annotations ["destructiveHint" "openWorldHint"] :typicalTokens 300}
  {:name "dispatch-dry-run" :description "Simulate one registered event and roll it back: the handler, interceptors and machine transitions run, every declared fx (child dispatches included) is recorded and skipped, then the pre-call frame-state is restored. Side effects a handler performs directly are NOT undone, downstream events are not simulated, and the simulation and its rollback stay visible in the epoch history. Trust the result only on :ok? true AND :rolled-back? true — :rollback-failed means simulated state can still be live. With epoch recording off it refuses before running, with :reason :no-epoch-recorded. Returns {:ok? true :dry-run? true :rolled-back? true :cascade-summary {...} :would-fire-effects [{:fx-id ... :args ...}] :db-state-after-simulation {...}}, the :cascade-summary in dispatch's shape. PRIVACY: :db-state-after-simulation is elided and redacted like get-path — `elision false` is honoured on every launch, `include-sensitive true` only under --allow-sensitive-reads. Every :would-fire-effects :args reads :rf/redacted by default; `include-fx-args true` opts them in, also only under --allow-sensitive-reads. Example: {:event \"[:cart/checkout]\"} -> {:ok? true :dry-run? true :rolled-back? true :cascade-summary {:event-id :cart/checkout :db-diff {...} ...} :would-fire-effects [{:fx-id :http :args :rf/redacted}] :db-state-after-simulation {...}}." :input-keys ["build" "cofx" "elision" "event" "frame" "include-fx-args" "include-sensitive" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["event"] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 800}
  {:name "eval-cljs" :description "Evaluate a ClojureScript form in the connected browser runtime (shadow-cljs cljs-eval) and return its EDN value as {:ok? true :value <v>}. Pass `frame` for any frame-scoped form (subscribe, dispatch): without it the form carries no frame, and such an op raises :rf.error/no-frame-context rather than falling back to :rf/default. The frame wrap is synchronous-lexical only, so an async continuation must capture the frame itself. `await true` awaits a returned Promise server-side (:rf.error/eval-cljs-rejected, or :rf.error/eval-cljs-timeout after `timeout-ms`). Enabled by default; a server launched with --no-eval returns :rf.error/eval-cljs-disabled. Example: {:form \"@(re-frame.core/subscribe [:current-user])\" :frame \":rf/default\"} -> {:ok? true :value {:id 42 :name \"Ada\"} :frame :rf/default}." :input-keys ["await" "build" "form" "frame" "max-tokens" "timeout-ms"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["form"] :output? true :annotations ["destructiveHint" "openWorldHint"] :typicalTokens 500}
  {:name "explain-render" :description "Per mounted Fresco boundary, which of its reads moved most recently and which retained event runs COULD have driven it (re-frame.fresco.tool/explain-render, folding Spec 009's retained event window at read time). No args. Two halves, never blended. PROVEN: :latest-reads (the reads standing at the boundary's :peak-epoch) and :snapshot (the exact number React compares), each :unknown where a row folds boundaries that do not share one read set and one number. LEADS: :candidates are retained runs that recomputed a subscription the boundary reads; no run can be joined to a re-render, so never present a candidate as the cause. :loss {:reason :uncorrelated} means the search ran and :candidates [] is an honest result; {:reason :cap} means the boundary's frames had an empty window (retention is :rf.trace/events-retained), so :candidates is :unknown. Whether the boundary then rendered is React's to know. Tool-frame boundaries are left out under :excluded; :reason :evidence-tier-unavailable or :evidence-tier-inactive when the door is not loaded or the build is production. Example: {} -> {:ok? true :schema :re-frame.fresco.evidence/v3 :explanations [{:boundary {...} :views [...] :frame :app/main :window {:frames [:app/main] :retained-runs 12} :snapshot 9 :peak-epoch 5 :latest-reads [{:sub-id :todo :query [:todo 7] :frame-id :app/main}] :loss {:reason :uncorrelated :dropped :unknown} :candidates [{:dispatch-id 41 :event-id :todo/toggle :frame-id :app/main :sub-id :todo}]}]}." :input-keys ["build" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 600}
  {:name "get-operating-frame" :description "Report the session's operating-frame state: :frames (every registered frame-id), :app-frames (the app frames, reserved :rf/* tool frames left out), :selected (the pin set-operating-frame made, or nil) and :operating (what frame-targeted ops resolve to; nil means AMBIGUOUS — two or more app frames and no pin, so an op without `frame` refuses). Read-only. Example: {} -> {:ok? true :frames [:rf/default :stories] :selected nil :operating nil}." :input-keys ["build" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 150}
  {:name "get-path" :description "Read the value at `path` (an EDN key vector, e.g. \"[:cart :items 0]\") in a frame's app-db; only that subtree crosses the wire. Returns {:ok? true :exists? true :path [...] :value <v>}, or {:ok? false :reason :path-not-found :deepest-valid-prefix [...]} so you can re-aim. `paths` reads several in one call, returning :results keyed by path; it excludes `path`. PRIVACY: the value crosses re-frame.core/project-egress under the :rf.egress/off-box-tool profile, so declared-sensitive paths read :rf/redacted and a value at or below a :large path becomes {:rf.size/large-elided {:path [...] :handle ...}}. To fetch it raw, re-call on the marker's :path — never the `:handle` vector, which get-path does not decode — with `elision false`, the size override, honoured on every launch. Sensitive paths still redact unless `include-sensitive true`, honoured only under --allow-sensitive-reads. A marker's :path addresses the CURRENT app-db, so following one out of a past epoch (trace-window, watch-epochs, snapshot :epochs, dispatch :trace / :settle) returns today's value. Read the past value with eval-cljs: `(if-let [r (re-frame2-pair.runtime/epoch-by-id 7 :my/app)] (get-in r [:db-after :doc :body]) {:epoch-unavailable 7})`, using :db-before when the marker came from that side. Example: {:path \"[:cart :items 99]\"} -> {:ok? false :reason :path-not-found :path [:cart :items 99] :deepest-valid-prefix [:cart :items]}." :input-keys ["build" "cache" "elision" "frame" "include-sensitive" "max-tokens" "path" "paths"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 500}
  {:name "get-re-frame2-pair-instructions" :description "Return the agent-onboarding text: routing rules for which tool to reach for, the EDN posture, the :origin :pair tagging convention and the wire-boundary pipeline. Call it at session start; it is inline, with no nREPL round-trip. It does not list the tools, which tools/list already carries. Returns {:ok? true :tool \"get-re-frame2-pair-instructions\" :tool-contract <string> :built-from <string> :text <string>}. :tool-contract fingerprints this server's tool names and argument keys: a value other than the one the re-frame2-pair skill states means a stale server, so rebuild and restart it before reading a missing tool as an app finding. :built-from is the git commit it was built from (\"unknown\" when the build recorded none); one that does not contain the newest server-source commit is stale too. Example: {} -> {:ok? true :tool \"get-re-frame2-pair-instructions\" :tool-contract \"30-1a2b3c4d\" :built-from \"<commit>\" :text \"re-frame2-pair-mcp agent quick reference...\"}." :input-keys ["cache" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 2000}
  {:name "handler-meta" :description "Return a registered handler's registration metadata: its source coord (:ns :file :line :column, plus an :rf.mcp/source-uri jump-to-editor link), :doc, :tags and any custom slots its reg-* macro emitted. Use it to find where an id is defined without an eval-cljs round-trip. `kind` takes list-handlers' kinds; `id` is EDN (\":user/login\"; an app-schema id is its path vector, \"[:user]\"). On kind event a dev build also returns :rf.handler/source, the registered form as a string, so you can read the body here; it reflects the CURRENT registration, not an older epoch's, and is absent in production builds and for other kinds. `frame` resolves the id through what that frame runs and adds its provenance (:rf.provenance/ns, :rf.image/coordinate); it is not valid with kind machine. Returns {:ok? true :kind k :id i ...meta...}, or :reason :not-registered. Example: {:kind \"event\" :id \":user/login\"} -> {:ok? true :kind :event :id :user/login :ns my.app.user :line 42 :file \"src/my/app/user.cljs\" :rf.mcp/source-uri \"file://...\" :rf.handler/source \"(reg-event :user/login ...)\"}." :input-keys ["build" "cache" "frame" "id" "kind" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["id" "kind"] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 400}
  {:name "list-handlers" :description "Return every id registered under one `kind` — the discovery surface; drill into one with handler-meta. Supported kinds: event, sub, fx, cofx, interceptor, view, route, head, error-projector, resource, mutation, resource-scope, app-schema, flow, http-interceptor, classification, machine — frame is not a kind (it returns :invalid-kind; read frames with rf/frame-ids). machine lists the event handlers flagged :rf/machine? true, and app-schema ids are app-db path vectors. `frame` lists only the ids that frame runs rather than the whole registrar; it is not valid with kind machine. Returns {:ok? true :kind k :ids [...] :count n}, the ids sorted so the list is stable across calls. Example: {:kind \"event\"} -> {:ok? true :kind :event :ids [:cart/add :cart/checkout :user/login ...] :count 47}." :input-keys ["build" "cache" "frame" "kind" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["kind"] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 800}
  {:name "list-subscriptions" :description "List the subscriptions live in a frame's sub-cache right now, read from the same source as snapshot's :sub-cache slice. The cache is ref-counted: an entry appears when a view subscribes and disappears when its last consumer disposes it. Returns {:ok? true :frame <id> :count N :subs [<query-v> ...]}, sorted. With `include-values true` each entry is {:query-v :value :ref-count :input-kind <:db|:static|:parametric> :realized-inputs [<query-v> ...]}, each :value elided and redacted as read-sub's is. Omit `frame` for the operating frame; a multi-frame session with none selected returns :ambiguous-frame. Example: {:include-values true} -> {:ok? true :frame :rf/default :count 1 :subs [{:query-v [:article/page :a1] :value {...} :ref-count 1 :input-kind :parametric :realized-inputs [[:article/by-id :a1] [:viewer/current]]}]}." :input-keys ["build" "cache" "elision" "frame" "include-sensitive" "include-values" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 400}
  {:name "orient" :description "App-shape orientation in ONE round-trip — \"what is this app and what can I drive?\" — composing what discover-app, snapshot, list-handlers and list-subscriptions would take several calls to gather. Returns {:ok? true :liveness {...} :frames {:all [...] :app [...] :operating <id>} :app-db-top-keys {<app-frame> [<key> ...]} :registry {:counts {<kind> N ...} :events [...] :subs [...] :fx [...]} :machines [...]}: registrar counts plus the first 20 ids of each list, never the full app-db, with reserved :rf/* tool frames split out. A list cut short appears in :truncated, whose :next is the list-handlers call that returns every id. Drill with list-handlers, handler-meta, get-path or read-sub. Example: {} -> {:ok? true :frames {:all [:rf/default :rf/xray] :app [:rf/default] :operating :rf/default} :app-db-top-keys {:rf/default [:cart :route :user]} :registry {:counts {:event 14 :sub 9 ...} :events [:cart/add :cart/checkout ...] ...} :machines [:checkout]}." :input-keys ["build" "cache" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 600}
  {:name "read-dom" :description "Query the RENDERED DOM by CSS `selector` and return what is on screen: {:ok? true :count N :truncated? bool :nodes [{:tag :text :attrs}]}. `sub-selector` narrows each match to its inner parts; `attrs` picks the attributes (default: a structural set plus every data-* and aria-* attribute). Bounded in the browser: at most `limit` nodes (default 50; :count is the full tally) and `max-text` characters per node (default 2000; longer text becomes an {:rf.size/large-elided {:type :dom-text ...}} marker). Read-only. PRIVACY: FAIL-OPEN — rendered text and attributes are not matched against app-db secrets, so a secret a view copied into the DOM ships raw. Classifying an app-db path never changes what a view renders, so the app must omit or mask content it does not want rendered. An ambiguous frame (several app frames, none pinned) fails closed with :reason :ambiguous-frame. Example: {:selector \"#app .counter\"} -> {:ok? true :count 1 :truncated? false :nodes [{:tag \"div\" :text \"Count: 3\" :attrs {\"class\" \"counter\" \"data-count\" \"3\"}}]}." :input-keys ["attrs" "build" "frame" "limit" "max-text" "max-tokens" "selector" "sub-selector"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["selector"] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 600}
  {:name "read-mounted-boundaries" :description "List every Fresco boundary mounted right now (re-frame.fresco.tool/read-mounted-boundaries). No args. Returns the evidence envelope {:ok? true :schema :re-frame.fresco.evidence/v3 :complete? ... :loss ... :boundaries [...]} — validate :schema first — where each boundary carries its :key, the :views that rendered it ({:view \"<ns>/<sym>\" :source {...}}, or :unknown for an unnamed body), :instances, :frame, :read-orders and its :reads ({:sub-id :query :frame-id :epoch}), never the values read. A boundary is keyed by its READ SET, the only identity the runtime keeps, so :instances counts the boundaries sharing one. The census is about subscription, not visibility: an empty roster means no boundary holds a live read edge, and a row does not prove the boundary is on screen. Boundaries under a reserved :rf/* tool frame are left out and reported under :excluded. Returns :reason :evidence-tier-unavailable when the door is not loaded, :evidence-tier-inactive in a production build. Example: {} -> {:ok? true :schema :re-frame.fresco.evidence/v3 :complete? true :boundaries [{:boundary {:parent nil :key [[:app/main :todo [:todo 7]]]} :views [{:view \"app.views/todo-row\" :source {...}}] :instances 3 :frame :app/main :reads [{:sub-id :todo :query [:todo 7] :frame-id :app/main :epoch 4}]}]}." :input-keys ["build" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 600}
  {:name "read-read-attribution" :description "For each subscription Fresco boundaries hold, the boundaries reading it — the exact reverse edge (re-frame.fresco.tool/read-read-attribution). No args. Returns the evidence envelope with :edges [{:sub-id :query :frame-id :epoch :fan-out N :readers [{:parent :key :views}]}]; each reader's :key is the one read-mounted-boundaries reports, so the two join directly. Exact without qualification. Start here when you can name a subscription and want the boundaries holding it, then take a :key to explain-render. A subscription nothing holds is absent, not listed with zero readers. Tool-frame subscriptions are left out under :excluded; :reason :evidence-tier-unavailable or :evidence-tier-inactive when the door is not loaded or the build is production. Example: {} -> {:ok? true :schema :re-frame.fresco.evidence/v3 :edges [{:sub-id :todo :query [:todo 7] :frame-id :app/main :epoch 4 :fan-out 3 :readers [{:parent nil :key [[:app/main :todo [:todo 7]]] :views [...]}]}]}." :input-keys ["build" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 500}
  {:name "read-recording" :description "Read back a recording's change-log, the companion to record. Returns {:ok? true :recording-id <id> :status :recording|:stopped :stopped-reason <kw|nil> :frames-sampled n :count n :entries [{:i <signal-index> :signal {...} :value v :t <ms> :frame <n>} ...]}: each entry is one CHANGE, and two signals that changed on the same paint share a :frame. `drain true` consumes the buffered entries so the next read sees only later ones, and the recording keeps running; `stop true` reads and closes. An unknown id returns :reason :no-such-recording. Example: {:recording-id \"rec-abc\" :stop true} -> {:ok? true :recording-id \"rec-abc\" :status :stopped :stopped-reason :ms :count 3 :entries [...]}." :input-keys ["build" "drain" "max-tokens" "recording-id" "stop"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["recording-id"] :output? true :annotations ["openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 800}
  {:name "read-sub" :description "Read a subscription's current value, validated. `sub` is an EDN query vector (\"[:user/by-id 42]\"); its id is checked against the live :sub registrar, and an unknown id returns :reason :unknown-id with :nearest matches and subscribes nothing, where a raw eval-cljs deref returns a silent nil. It subscribes on demand, so it reads subs no view has mounted. A throwing sub returns :reason :sub-error; non-vector input :not-a-sub-vector, unreadable input :invalid-sub-edn. Omit `frame` for the operating frame; a multi-frame session with none selected returns :ambiguous-frame. Returns {:ok? true :query-v [...] :frame <id> :value <v>}. PRIVACY: the value crosses re-frame.core/project-egress, so declared-sensitive values read :rf/redacted and large ones elide to :rf.size/large-elided; `elision false` (honoured on every launch) shows a large value raw, `include-sensitive true` (only under --allow-sensitive-reads) a sensitive one. Example: {:sub \"[:current-userr]\"} -> {:ok? false :reason :unknown-id :kind :sub :id :current-userr :nearest [:current-user] :subscribed? false :hint \"unknown :sub :current-userr; did you mean :current-user?\"}." :input-keys ["build" "cache" "elision" "frame" "include-sensitive" "max-tokens" "sub"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["sub"] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 400}
  {:name "read-ui" :description "Return the rendered subtree AND the re-frame2 view that produced it, in one round-trip and with no test ids: it reads the data-rf-view and data-rf2-source-coord attributes the adapter stamps on each view root (Spec 006 §View tagging contract). Pass exactly one entry point — `view-id`, `point` {x y} (the view under it) or `selector` — with that precedence. Returns {:ok? true :via <entry> :entity {:view-id :source-coord {:ns :handler-id :line :col :file} :render-key :subs-read [...]} :content {:tag :text :attrs}}, where :subs-read is the frame's live sub query-vectors. A node with no tagged view root returns :entity {:view-id nil :reason :no-tagged-view-root}; no entry point returns :reason :no-target-arg, nothing matched :no-element. Read-only. PRIVACY: FAIL-OPEN like read-dom — :content is not matched against app-db secrets, so a secret a view copied into the DOM ships raw, and classifying its app-db path does not change the render: the app must omit or mask content it does not want rendered. `max-text` (default 2000) elides long text, and an ambiguous frame fails closed with :reason :ambiguous-frame. Example: {:view-id \":my.app/counter\"} -> {:ok? true :via :view-id :entity {:view-id :my.app/counter :source-coord {:ns \"my.app\" :handler-id \"counter\" :line 42 :col 3 :file \"/abs/my/app.cljs\"} :render-key 8123 :subs-read [[:count]]} :content {:tag \"div\" :text \"Count: 3\" :attrs {\"class\" \"counter\"}}}." :input-keys ["build" "frame" "max-text" "max-tokens" "point" "selector" "view-id"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 700}
  {:name "record" :description "Start a background, read-only recorder over one or more `signals` and return at once with a :recording-id; let the human interact, then read the change-log with read-recording. It catches intermittent and human-in-the-loop bugs: each signal is sampled once per animation frame and only CHANGES are logged, each with :t and a frame counter. Signals: {:app-db [path]}, {:sub [query-v]}, {:dom \"sel\"} (optionally :attr \"name\"), {:focus true} (document.activeElement). `stop` ends it at the first of :ms (default 30000), :changes (entry count) or :pred, a data predicate over the sample map {<signal-index> <value>}: {:signal 0 :equals v}, {:signal 0 :changed true}, {:signal 0 :path [...] :equals v} or {:signal 0 :contains \"s\"}. PRIVACY: :app-db / :sub samples cross re-frame.core/project-egress at sample time, so sensitive leaves redact and large slots elide; `elision false` (honoured on every launch) records large values raw, `include-sensitive true` (only under --allow-sensitive-reads) sensitive ones. :dom / :focus samples are FAIL-OPEN: a secret a view copied into the DOM ships raw, and classifying its app-db path does not change the render, so the app must omit or mask content it does not want rendered. An :app-db / :sub signal with an ambiguous frame fails closed with :reason :ambiguous-frame. Example: {:signals \"[{:focus true} {:dom \\\"#count\\\"}]\" :stop {:ms 15000}} -> {:ok? true :recording-id \"rec-<uuid>\" :signals [{:focus true} {:dom \"#count\"}] :frame :rf/default :stop {:ms 15000}}." :input-keys ["build" "elision" "frame" "include-sensitive" "max-entries" "max-tokens" "signals" "stop"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["signals"] :output? true :annotations ["openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 200}
  {:name "replace-app-db" :description "State injection: replace a frame's app-db with an arbitrary EDN value, such as a captured bug-repro state. `db` is parsed as EDN data, never source. It bypasses the dispatch loop and fires no fx, recording a synthetic :rf.epoch/db-replaced epoch, so restore-epoch on that epoch with `to \"before\"` undoes the injection. Returns {:ok? true :frame <id> :epoch-id <id> :cascade-summary {:event-id :rf.epoch/db-replaced :db-diff {...} :fx-fired []}}; no such frame, a drain in flight or an app-schema mismatch returns :reason :reset-rejected with the app-db unchanged. GATED: without --allow-writes (default off) it returns :reason :rf.error/writes-disabled and touches nothing. Example: {:db \"{:cart {:items []} :user nil}\"} -> {:ok? true :frame :rf/default :epoch-id 42 :cascade-summary {...}}." :input-keys ["build" "db" "frame" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["db"] :output? true :annotations ["destructiveHint" "openWorldHint"] :typicalTokens 150}
  {:name "replay-epoch" :description "Strict replay of a retained epoch in ONE call: re-drive its recorded event through the app's own handlers with its raw trigger event, its recorded :rf.cofx under the strict mint policy and both recorded override maps, all resolved in-process from the ring — only the id crosses the wire. It runs against the frame's CURRENT state and code and does NOT restore first (restore-epoch, then replay, to rewind): external effects fire AGAIN, and the replay records a NEW epoch tagged :origin :pair. A fact the current code needs that the record lacks fails :rf.error/missing-required-cofx rather than being minted. Returns dispatch's consequence shape for the new epoch, plus :replayed? true and :source-epoch-id. Refused before anything dispatches: an unknown or aged-out id (:rf.epoch/replay-unknown-epoch), a drain in flight (:rf.epoch/replay-during-drain), a halted, synthetic or incomplete record (:rf.epoch/replay-non-replayable-record), a recorded fn-valued override (:rf.epoch/replay-unreplayable-fx-override). Not --allow-writes-gated. `epoch-id` is EDN, so pass \"7\". Example: {:epoch-id \"7\"} -> {:ok? true :replayed? true :source-epoch-id 7 :epoch-id 12 :db-changed? true :effects-fired [:http] :cascade-summary {...}}." :input-keys ["build" "epoch-id" "frame" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["epoch-id"] :output? true :annotations ["destructiveHint" "openWorldHint"] :typicalTokens 200}
  {:name "reset-operating-frame" :description "Clear the operating-frame pin set by set-operating-frame. Frame-targeted ops then resolve to the sole app frame, or refuse with :ambiguous-frame when there are two or more and no per-call `frame`. Idempotent. Returns {:ok? true :frames [...] :selected nil :operating <resolved-or-nil>}. Example: {} -> {:ok? true :frames [:rf/default :stories] :selected nil :operating nil}." :input-keys ["build" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint"] :typicalTokens 150}
  {:name "restore-epoch" :description "Time-travel undo: atomically rewind a frame's whole frame-state — BOTH the app-db and runtime-db partitions — to a retained epoch's :frame-state-after, or with `to \"before\"` to its :frame-state-before, which undoes that epoch (a replace-app-db injection included). Find ids with trace-window, watch-epochs or snapshot's :epochs slice; `epoch-id` is EDN, so the integer id 7 is passed as \"7\". Returns {:ok? true :restored? true :epoch-id 7 :cascade-summary {... :restore? true} :unreplayable-effects [...]}, where :unreplayable-effects lists the fx the original cascade fired that a restore cannot undo (HTTP requests, navigation, persisted writes). A refused restore (an id not in the ring, a drain in flight) returns :restored? false with the framework's :reason, e.g. :rf.epoch/restore-unknown-epoch, and the state unchanged. GATED: without --allow-writes (default off) it returns :reason :rf.error/writes-disabled and touches nothing. Example: {:epoch-id \"7\"} -> {:ok? true :restored? true :epoch-id 7 :cascade-summary {:event-id :cart/add :restore? true ...} :unreplayable-effects [{:fx-id :http}]}." :input-keys ["build" "epoch-id" "frame" "max-tokens" "to"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["epoch-id"] :output? true :annotations ["destructiveHint" "openWorldHint"] :typicalTokens 150}
  {:name "set-operating-frame" :description "Pin the session's operating frame: the frame that frame-targeted ops (dispatch, snapshot, get-path, read-sub, …) use when no per-call `frame` is passed. With two or more app frames and no pin those ops refuse with :ambiguous-frame; pin once and they resolve here until reset-operating-frame, or a runtime reload, clears it. `frame` must name a registered frame (bare \"stories\" or \":stories\"). Returns {:ok? true :frames [...] :app-frames [...] :selected <pinned> :operating <resolved>}, so you can confirm the pin took. Examples: 1. Pin a frame: {:frame \":stories\"} -> {:ok? true :frames [:rf/default :stories] :selected :stories :operating :stories ...}. 2. Unknown frame: {:frame \":nope\"} -> {:ok? false :reason :no-such-frame :frame :nope :frames [:rf/default :stories]}. 3. Missing frame: {} -> {:ok? false :reason :missing-frame}." :input-keys ["build" "frame" "max-tokens"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["frame"] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint"] :typicalTokens 200}
  {:name "snapshot" :description "Coarse per-frame state read in one round-trip: a map keyed by frame-id carrying the requested slices — :app-db, :sub-cache, :machines, :epochs, :traces (`include` narrows them). By default every rich slice is an {:rf.mcp/summary {:type :keys :count :bytes}} marker, so a discovery read fits the wire cap; expand with `mode \"full\"`, per-slice `modes`, or for :app-db a `path` (an EDN key vector, returning that subtree). The default scope is APP frames: reserved :rf/* tool frames are left out with a :note naming them — pass `frames \"all\"`, or name one, to include it. PRIVACY: :app-db and :sub-cache cross re-frame.core/project-egress under the :rf.egress/off-box-tool profile, so declared-sensitive paths read :rf/redacted and large slots become :rf.size/large-elided markers. `elision false` lifts only the size axis and is honoured on every launch; `include-sensitive true` lifts the sensitive axis, honoured only when the server was launched with --allow-sensitive-reads. The :machines slice is runtime-db state, classified by each `reg-machine` definition, and FAILS CLOSED to :rf/redacted by default; `include-sensitive true` under the same gate ships the live snapshots through the projection. :epochs records and :traces events cross the epoch projector: ids, flags, counts and timings ship, while event and effect arguments read :rf/redacted. :db-after is diff-encoded unless `epochs-mode \"full\"`, and :epochs dedup unless `dedup false`. Example: {:include [\"app-db\"] :path \"[:cart :items]\"} -> {:ok? true :mode :path-sliced :path [:cart :items] :snapshot {:rf/default {:app-db [{:sku \"x\" :qty 2}]}}}." :input-keys ["build" "cache" "dedup" "elision" "epochs-mode" "frames" "include" "include-sensitive" "max-tokens" "mode" "modes" "path"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["idempotentHint" "openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 3000}
  {:name "tail-build" :description "Wait for a hot reload to land by polling a probe form until its value differs from a PRE-EDIT baseline. Order matters: evaluate the probe with eval-cljs BEFORE the source edit and keep its printed value, make the edit, then call with the same `probe` and that value as `baseline` (pr-str rendering). `baseline` is required with `probe` (:reason :missing-baseline), because only a pre-edit value recognises a reload that landed before the first sample. Pick a probe whose value your edit changes, such as a handler-meta :line. Returns {:ok? true :soft? false :probe-values {:baseline ... :initial ... :final ...}}, or :reason :timed-out after `wait-ms` (default 5000); with no probe it is a 300ms soft delay, {:ok? true :soft? true}. The probe is evaluated as CLJS, so a server launched with --no-eval refuses it with :rf.error/eval-cljs-disabled. Example: {:probe \"(my.app/build-marker)\" :baseline \"41\"} -> {:ok? true :soft? false :probe-values {:baseline \"41\" :initial 42 :final 42}}." :input-keys ["baseline" "build" "max-tokens" "probe" "wait-ms"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["destructiveHint" "openWorldHint"] :typicalTokens 100}
  {:name "trace-window" :description "Return the :rf/epoch-records added in the last `ms` milliseconds (default 1000) for the operating frame. Every record crosses re-frame.core/project-egress: declared-sensitive slots read :rf/redacted (`include-sensitive true` lifts that, only under --allow-sensitive-reads), and a record that held classified material ships labelled :rf.epoch/sensitive? true. :db-after is diff-encoded against :db-before unless `epochs-mode \"full\"`, and the vector is structurally deduped unless `dedup false`. An :rf.size/large-elided marker's :path addresses the CURRENT app-db, so get-path on it returns today's value — read the past value with the eval-cljs recipe in get-path's description. A page holds at most `limit` records (default 50), cut further to what fits `max-tokens` (:truncated-by :max-tokens); while :has-more? is true, pass :next-cursor back as `cursor` (an aged-out cursor returns :reason :rf.mcp/cursor-stale). A record too large for any page returns an empty page with :advisory {:reason :record-exceeds-max-tokens :record-tokens N}: re-call with `max-tokens` 0 or at least N. Example: {:ms 5000 :limit 20} -> {:ok? true :count 20 :epochs [...] :has-more? true :next-cursor \"<b64>\"}." :input-keys ["build" "cache" "cursor" "dedup" "epochs-mode" "frame" "include-sensitive" "limit" "max-tokens" "ms"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 4800}
  {:name "watch-epochs" :description "Pull-mode poll: the epochs matching `pred` that landed after `since-id`, the :head-id a previous poll returned; call repeatedly to watch. `pred` keys: :event-id, :event-id-prefix, :effects, :touches-path, :sub-ran, :render, :origin, :frame, and :timing-ms (a number meaning >= N, or a comparison string: \">100\", \">=100\", \"<200\", \"<=50\", \"=42\"), all applied server-side. Returns {:ok? true :matches [...] :count n :head-id <id>}. Egress, encoding and paging match trace-window: records cross re-frame.core/project-egress (declared-sensitive slots read :rf/redacted; `include-sensitive true` lifts that, only under --allow-sensitive-reads), :db-after is diff-encoded unless `epochs-mode \"full\"`, and matches dedup unless `dedup false`. An :rf.size/large-elided marker's :path addresses the CURRENT app-db, so get-path returns today's value; get-path's description has the recipe for the past one. A page holds at most `limit` matches (default 50), cut to `max-tokens`; while :has-more? is true, pass :next-cursor back as `cursor`, which overrides `since-id` (aged out: :reason :rf.mcp/cursor-stale). An empty page carrying :advisory {:reason :record-exceeds-max-tokens :record-tokens N} names the budget one record needs. Example: {:since-id \"42\" :pred {:effects :http}} -> {:ok? true :matches [...] :count 2 :head-id 47}." :input-keys ["build" "cache" "cursor" "dedup" "epochs-mode" "frame" "include-sensitive" "limit" "max-tokens" "pred" "since-id"] :gated-input-keys [] :required [] :output? true :annotations ["openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 4800}
  {:name "watch-until" :description "Block until a data predicate over `signals` holds, or `timeout-ms` (default 30000) elapses — the blocking counterpart to record; the server polls a runtime read about every 100ms. Signals use record's vocabulary, and `pred` is a data predicate over the sample map {<signal-index> <value>}: {:signal 0 :equals v}, {:signal 0 :changed true}, {:signal 0 :path [...] :equals v}, {:signal 0 :contains \"s\"} or {:signal 0} (any non-nil). Returns {:ok? true :held? true :elapsed-ms n :sample {...} :t <ms>}, or on timeout {:ok? false :reason :watch-timeout :timed-out? true :last-sample {...}}, showing how close it got. Read-only. PRIVACY: :app-db / :sub samples cross re-frame.core/project-egress BEFORE the predicate tests them, so a value at a classified path compares as its marker and cannot trip the watch — aim at an unclassified value or lift the axis: `elision false` (honoured on every launch) for large values, `include-sensitive true` (only under --allow-sensitive-reads) for sensitive ones. :dom / :focus samples are FAIL-OPEN, as in record. Example: {:signals \"[{:app-db [:upload :status]}]\" :pred {:signal 0 :equals :done}} -> {:ok? true :held? true :elapsed-ms 4200 :sample {0 :done} :t 1712...}." :input-keys ["build" "elision" "frame" "include-sensitive" "max-tokens" "pred" "signals" "timeout-ms"] :gated-input-keys [] :required ["pred" "signals"] :output? true :annotations ["openWorldHint" "readOnlyHint"] :typicalTokens 200}]}
